{"subjectId":"dsa-advanced","id":"bit_magic","title":"Bit Magic","group":"","order":1,"html":"<h4>Binary Representation of Negative Numbers</h4>\n<p>-&gt; Negative numbers are represented in 2's complement form</p>\n<p>-&gt; Range of numbers : [-2^(n-1) to 2^(n-1)-1] : n - no.of bits</p>\n<p>-&gt; Steps to get 2's Complement</p>\n<p>1. Invert all Bits</p>\n<p>2. Add 1</p>\n<p>Direct Formula = 2^n - x</p>\n<p>--------------------</p>\n<p>Example : n = 4</p>\n<p>Range : [-2^3 to 2^3 - 1]</p>\n<p>Binary Representation of x = 3</p>\n<p>3 : 0011</p>\n<p>2's complement = (1100 + 1) = 1101</p>\n<p>Why 2's complement form..?</p>\n<p>1. we have only one representation of zero</p>\n<p>2. The arithematic operations are easier to perform. Actually 2's complement form is dervied from the idea of 0-x</p>\n<p>3. The leading bit is always 1</p>\n<h4>Bitwise Operations</h4>\n<p>-&gt; Decimal to Binary </p>\n<p>-&gt; Binary to Decimal</p>\n<p>-&gt; Bitwise Operator's:</p>\n<p> -&gt; Bitwise AND : &amp;</p>\n<p> -&gt; Bitwise OR : |</p>\n<p> -&gt; Bitwise XOR : ^</p>\n<p> -&gt; Left shift Operator (Zero inserted at left side)</p>\n<p> -&gt; Right Shift Operator (Zero inserted at Right side)</p>\n<p> -&gt; Bitwise NOT : ~</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3>Check the kth bit is set or not</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> n = 5; k = 1<br><strong>Output:</strong> Yes</p>\n</div>\n<p>k &lt;= No.of bits in Binary Representation</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def iskth(n, k):\n  if n &amp; (1&lt;&lt;(k-1)):\n    print(\"SET\")\n  else:\n    print(\"NOT SET\")\n\niskth(5, 3)\niskth(5, 2)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def iskth(n, k):\n  if n &gt;&gt; (k-1) &amp; 1:\n    print(\"SET\")\n  else:\n    print(\"NOT SET\")\n\niskth(5, 3)\niskth(5, 2)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Count Set Bits</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> n = 5<br><strong>Output:</strong> 2</p>\n</div>\n<p>Naive Solution</p>\n<p>LSB : Least Significant Bit</p>\n<p>MSB : Most Significant Bit</p>\n<p>1. Initialize res = 0</p>\n<p>2. Traverse through all bits from LSB to MSB and increment res for set bits</p>\n<p>3. return res</p>\n<p>Hint : we get LSB of n using n%2</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Solution\n\ndef countBits(n):\n  res = 0\n  while n:\n    if n%2 == 1:\n      res += 1\n    n = n//2\n\n  return res\n\n\ncountBits(9)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient Solution : Brian Kernigam's Algorithm\n\n# Idea : Traverse only through set bits\n\ndef countBits(n):\n  res = 0\n  while n:\n    n = n &amp; (n-1)\n    res = res + 1      # This Expression should make last set bit as 0\n\n  return res\n\n\ncountBits(9)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Lookup Table Solution</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Constant Time Preprocessing</li>\n<li>Works for 32 bit numbers</li>\n</ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>n = 13\n\ntbl = [0] * 256\n\ndef initialize():\n  for i in range(256):\n    tbl[i] = (i&amp;1) + tbl[i//2]\n\ndef countsetBits(n):\n    return tbl[n &amp; 0xff] + tbl[(n &gt;&gt; 8) &amp; 0xff] + tbl[(n &gt;&gt; 16) &amp; 0xff] + tbl[(n &gt;&gt; 24) &amp; 0xff]\n\ninitialize()\ncountsetBits(n)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Find the Only Odd</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> l = [10, 30, 30, 10, 30, 30, 20]<br><strong>Output:</strong> 20</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Method 1 (Simple)\n\n# Traverse every element in list\n\ndef findOdd(l):\n  res = None\n  for x in l:\n    count = l.count(x)\n    if count%2 != 0:\n      res = x\n      break\n  return res\n\nl = [10, 10, 20, 20, 30]\nfindOdd(l)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Method 2 (Bitwise XOR)\n'''\n\nXOR Properties\n    X^0 = X\n    X^X = X\n\n'''\n\nl = [10, 10, 20, 20, 30]\n\ndef findOdd(l):\n  res = 0\n  for x in l:\n    res = res^x\n\n  return res\n\nfindOdd(l)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Power of 2</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> n = 4<br><strong>Output:</strong> Yes</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Solution\n\ndef pow(n):\n  if n == 0:\n    return False\n  while n != 1:\n    if n%2 != 0:\n      return False\n    n = n//2\n  return True\n\npow(4)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient Solution\n\ndef pow(n):\n  if n == 0:\n    return False\n  return (n &amp; (n-1) == 0)\n\npow(3)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Find the Only One Odd Occuring</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> [4, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]<br><strong>Output:</strong> 3</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Solution\n\ndef findoneOdd(arr):\n  for i in arr:\n    c = 0\n    for j in arr:\n      if i == j:\n        c += 1\n    if c%2 != 0:\n      return i\n\nfindoneOdd([4, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5])</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient Solution\n\ndef findoneOdd(arr):\n  res = 0\n  for i in arr:\n    res = res ^ i\n  return res\n\nfindoneOdd([4, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5])</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Find the Two Odd occuring Elements</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> arr = [3, 4, 3, 4, 5, 4, 4, 6, 7, 7]<br><strong>Output:</strong> 5 6</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Solution\n\ndef OddApp(arr):\n  for i in arr:\n    c = 0\n    for j in arr:\n      if i == j:\n        c += 1\n    if c%2 != 0:\n      print(i, end = \" \")\n\nOddApp([3, 4, 3, 4, 5, 4, 4, 6, 7, 7])</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient Solution\n\ndef OddApp(arr):\n  xors = 0\n  res1 = 0\n  res2 = 0\n\n  for i in arr:\n    xors = xors ^ i\n\n  sn = xors &amp; ~(xors - 1)\n\n  for i in arr:\n    if i &amp; sn != 0:\n      res1 = res1 ^ i\n    else:\n      res2 = res2 ^ i\n\n  print(res1, res2)\n\nOddApp([3, 4, 3, 4, 5, 4, 4, 6, 7, 7])</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Power Set using Bitwise</h3>\n<p>s = \"abc\", n = 3</p>\n<p>we consider binary representations of numbers from 0 to 7</p>\n<p> 0 0 0 0 \" \" </p>\n<p> 1 0 0 1 \"a\"</p>\n<p> 2 0 1 0 \"b\"</p>\n<p> 3 0 1 1 \"ab\"</p>\n<p> 4 1 0 0 \"c\"</p>\n<p> 5 1 0 1 \"ac\"</p>\n<p> 6 1 1 0 \"bc\"</p>\n<p> 7 1 1 1 \"abc\"</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def printPowset(s):\n  n = len(s)\n  psize = (1 &lt;&lt; n)\n\n  for i in range(psize):\n    for j in range(n):\n      if ((i &amp; (1 &lt;&lt; j)) != 0):\n        print(s[j], end = \" \")\n\n    print()</code></pre></figure></section>"}